{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-148",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "One combined diagram of four convenient rays from A to a concave spherical mirror and the downward image A_1B_1. O means curvature center. First-order paraxial convergence, not exact spherical focusing. Vertical scale is explicitly enlarged. The lower contact is labeled C by authoritative text; distinguishing its source C terminal from a prime remains HOLD.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-148.png",
      "sha256": "e69ddc9f8bbeffa3e2b8ed3738f8ac513a7ea90af618ee29f9147c38a0a0b23c"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "If the position of an object relative to a spherical mirror is known, a ray construction can locate its **image**.\nFirst construct the image of an individual point, using a ruler and compass to determine the reflected ray paths.\nThe focusing constructions below use the paraxial approximation."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "To construct the image of an object point $A$, use the following convenient rays (Figure 148)."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "1. The ray $AOC$ through the center of curvature $O$ retraces the same straight path $COA$ after reflection."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "2. The ray $AFK$ through the focus is reflected parallel to the principal axis."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "3. The ray $AP$ strikes the pole of the mirror; its reflected ray is symmetric to the incident ray about the principal axis."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "4. The incident ray $AE$ is parallel to the principal axis; its reflected path $EFA_1$ passes through the focus."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "In the paraxial imaging model shown, rays from $A$ that reflect from the mirror pass through the image point $A_1$.\nChoose **any two** of the convenient rays to locate $A_1$.\nThe other reflected rays pass through the same image point.\n\nA real mirror may not reflect every convenient ray.\nFor example, some medical concave mirrors have a hole at the center, so a ray reaching the hole is not reflected.\nThe image can still be constructed using rays reflected by the remaining reflecting surface."},
      {"lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n71", "text": "To construct the image of an entire object, it is enough to construct the images of points on its **outline**."}
    ],
    "inventory": [
